From afe766fc7a16f00bb57aa126c5a319e3e3cd0ebf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:51:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix: what no layout could place when its display went is
 not sent to the corner
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Nothing docked, a window on the monitor, its app hidden or the window in the Dock. The monitor's cable is pulled, and Arbor
took the window to the corner of the laptop's display: it jumped into view when it showed, and stayed in the corner if
Arbor ended meanwhile. Plugged in again, the monitor had it in its own corner. A window told to stay in view went too, if
the laptop's view was held and showed nothing. Seen on a hosted Mac, to the point: (1023,767) on a display of 1024×768.

Whatever changes display counts as out of view until a layout has placed it. No layout places a window that is off the
screen, and a held view that shows nothing lets no layout through. The last step of a change of displays then sent
whatever still counted to the corner.

- What this change has sent to another display, belongs in view there by the layout in effect, and no layout placed is
  put where it floats, then and there, where a layout would put it. macOS itself leaves such a window where its display was
  until it shows; it obeys a move while it is away. One move, which nobody sees, and none when it shows. A held view that
  shows nothing takes in only what was told to stay in view. What is off the screen is never raised: that would fetch
  it out of the Dock.
- Where macOS has brought a floating window, at its own size and most of it on the display that takes it in, is where
  it is remembered. Arbor worked out a frame of its own and moved the window a second time for the difference: on that Mac
  1.6 s after macOS, from (220,180) to (120,140).

Two tests held only through the mark this takes away, and say so now: a floating window that was in the Dock when its
display went comes out where it went in, as on one display, and stays where macOS brought it; and the quit that fetches a
window whatever the books say gets its window out of view by an app that does not obey. Both scripts of the known failure
`left-its-place` are cured; the rule still breaks by another road, with this change and without, and the entry tells that one.
