From b62afe25a6cd8217b62a58eb6b35982661ce2e70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 07:13:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] test(mac-shell): off the screen when its display goes, on
 a real Mac

A floating window whose app is hidden, or that is in the Dock, while a virtual second display is unplugged and plugged in
again, with nothing docked. It claims what the change before this one promises: the window is not in the corner, lies on the
display it belongs to, and nothing moves when it shows. And it measures what nobody had recorded: what macOS does to such a
window by itself, with Arbor and without, and whether such a window obeys a move. Every change is recorded as a trace.

It has run twice on hosted Macs (macOS 26.6.2), within ten minutes: with the change 40 of 40; without it 30 of 40, all ten the
fault. What it measured is in the ledger: a window that is hidden or in the Dock obeys a move, which settles a case that
was open; and macOS alone does not bring such a window over when its display goes, which is a new one.

The fake Mac has one display, so like check-displays.sh it cannot be rehearsed to its end. A copy with display_add and
display_remove stubbed was rehearsed instead, which found that the second app was never started.
